# Automatic reviewer assignment on pull requests.
# Later patterns override earlier ones.
#
# Two kinds of repository carry this file, and they need different values
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# This one is an **instance**: a series run by an Editorial Board, where a
# review request goes to that board's own team, and the setup below is how
# that team is named.
#
# The **product** -- the public repository this code is published from --
# has no Editorial Board and no organisation team to name. One maintainer,
# and the catch-all rule carries that maintainer's own account: the whole
# of what this file earns there is that an outside contributor's pull
# request reaches them without anybody having to watch the repository.
#
# That line is maintained by hand here, and held by
# `tools/tests/declaration/test_published.py::
# test_the_literals_that_cannot_read_the_declaration_still_agree_with_it`,
# which reads both shapes this file can correctly take. Nothing writes it.
# It used to be written by the tool that produced the product from its
# source, out of the account that tool was run as -- that tool is not part
# of this repository and there is nothing here to run, so a reader looking
# for it would have been looking for something that does not exist.
#
# Which is what a duplicate finds when it first opens this file: a single
# account on the catch-all rather than a team, belonging to somebody who
# is not a collaborator on the duplicate's own repository and cannot be
# assigned a review there. Replacing it is step 2 below.
#
# One-off setup, once per instance, and stated here rather than pointed at:
# this file used to send a reader to a TODO.md that no longer exists, so
# the only guidance it carried pointed at nothing.
#
# 1. Create an organisation team and add the Board members to it. Name it
#    `editorial-board`, exactly: that slug is the one `app/src/auth/role.ts`
#    asks GitHub about when it decides who signs in as a Board member, so a
#    team under any other name leaves every member falling back to
#    instance/data/config.yml alone.
# 2. Write it below as `@<organisation>/editorial-board`, where
#    `<organisation>` is the owner half of `instance/config.json`'s
#    `identity.repository`.
#
# The literal has to exist because GitHub reads this file verbatim, before
# any code of this project's can run -- it is one of the six files a
# duplicate edits by hand, and README.md's "What a duplicate edits"
# enumerates all six rather than leaving the count to be trusted: three
# the boundary declares, two relay configurations, and this file. It
# cannot drift: `tools/tests/declaration/test_published.py::
# test_the_literals_that_cannot_read_the_declaration_still_agree_with_it`
# refuses a team in any organisation other than the declared one, in
# either shape, and requires the team itself wherever an instance is
# declared at all.
#
# Longer form, with the rest of the board's own setup:
# docs/operating/operations.md, "Board notifications", step 2.

# Default: handbook, governance, and app changes are reviewed by the Editorial Board.
